<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1510">1510</a>–<a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon was a French naval officer and Knight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> who in 1555, backed by Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspard-de-coligny">Gaspard de Coligny</a>, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Antarctique in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanabara-bay">Guanabara Bay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-coligny">Fort Coligny</a> on an island, but religious quarrels with Calvinist settlers divided the colony and he returned to France in 1559. The Portuguese destroyed it and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolas-durand-de-villegaignon.jpg" alt="Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Lagneau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_Lagneau_-_Nicolas_Durand_de_Villegagnon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Villegaignon was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/provins">Provins</a>, was educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and took a law degree at Orleans. In 1531 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights of Malta</a>, whose grand master was his uncle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-villiers-de-lisle-adam">Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam</a>. Although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-ottoman-alliance">Franco-Ottoman alliance</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> itself out of direct war with the Turks, he fought for the Order, which sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. He served in the fleet gathered off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1534 for the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a>, and was wounded in the expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1541.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1548 he commanded the French galleys that carried the five-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-queen-of-scots">Mary, Queen of Scots</a>, promised to the Dauphin, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, sailing round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> while the English fleet expected them from the other direction, and landing at Roscoff. Back in Scotland in February 1549 he helped take Ferniehirst Castle from the English and argued for a fortification at Roxburgh. Commanding the Knights in Mdina, he helped repel the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 1551, was present at the Siege of Tripoli, wrote an account of it in 1553 and defended the defeated commander, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspard-de-vallier">Gaspard de Vallier</a>, against the Grand Master d'Homedes.</p><p>The colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> began with two ships and 600 soldiers and colonists, mainly French Huguenots and Swiss Calvinists. Besides giving them a refuge, he hoped to exploit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilwood">brazilwood</a>, a valuable source of red dye and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and to search for precious metals and stones. A dispute over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> led him to expel those who held Calvin's view from his fortified island. He left the colony under his nephew Bois-le-Comte while seeking funds and ships, and after its fall gave up his claims for 30,000 écus from the Portuguese Crown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1560 he challenged Calvin to a debate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, which Calvin declined, and he took part in the repression of the Amboise conspiracy. Pierre Richier attacked his conduct in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in a pamphlet of 1561. From 1568 he represented the Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> at the French court, and he died on 9 January 1571 as commander of the Order's commandery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a>. The quarrel continued in print, with André Thévet's account of 1572 and Jean Léry's reply of 1578. Villegagnon Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> and a street in Mdina bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicolas Durand de Villegagnon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1555 the French admiral <b>Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon</b>, backed by Admiral Gaspard de Coligny, sailed to Guanabara Bay in Brazil with some 600 settlers.</blockquote><a href="/state/france-antarctique">France Antarctique</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/provins">Provins</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.56,3.3&amp;year=1549" aria-label="Provins on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4626 467 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5076 737h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5076 737h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.56,3.3&amp;year=1549">What was here in 1549? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
